findings.description:
UncertaintyTransfer.__post_init__ checks only that `uncertainty == propagate(transfer, source_uncertainty)`, so the propagated record is self-consistent with whatever source was handed in. Nothing ties that source uncertainty to the transfer's source quantity, source record or value:
(a) an INTERVAL of [10, 11] K is propagated for a transfer whose value is 350 K and round-trips;
(b) the propagated `source` is overwritten with `transfer:<source_record_id>`, so an uncertainty taken from 'some-other-quantity' now reads as coming from the transfer's source run;
(c) a 1e-6 K NUMERICAL uncertainty from another run is accepted;
(d) propagate_uncertainty_chain checks only name connectivity and equal instants, so consecutive transfers carrying different values (350 K, then 400 K) propagate the same 2 K.
With a conversion, the efficiency is treated as exact while the output keeps kind STANDARD; the only sign that efficiency uncertainty is missing is a note. The module's stated purpose is to replace a parallel dictionary keyed by a coincidentally matching name, but in practice it is one: the core cannot tell an uncertainty that belongs to the crossing from one that does not. No src module calls it, so every production crossing still carries a bare value (see the residual CORE-016 finding).

