verification.reason:
Reproduced at 294c129 (engcore imported from src). The finding is real. One correction: the title says "any" check, but the code uses all() over the checks present (evaluation.py:167-172), so one failed check does exclude a candidate. The rest holds. `best()` (experiment.py:200-207) accepts `is_feasible is True` without comparing the checked constraint names to `self.constraints`. `ConstraintCheck` (constraints.py:58-91) has no `__post_init__`, so nothing ties `satisfied` to `margin` or `value`, or `value` to `result.values[metric]`. Four cases get an infeasible candidate ranked best, and each survives a `ScientificExperiment` to_dict/from_dict round trip:
(A) a genuine `ConstraintDefinition.check` on only one of two declared constraints, while the result carries T_hot=900 K against a 400 K ceiling;
(B) genuine checks run on a value that is not the result's (checked 0.1 A, result 3 A);
(C) one check naming an undeclared constraint;
(D) `satisfied=True` with a negative margin and a violating value.
Controls behave correctly: honest violating checks and an evaluation with no checks are both excluded.

This is new, not residual. The audit doc says CORE-015 ranks only candidates "with declared constraints checked and satisfied", and its only stated residual is that unassessed evaluations are "recorded, not ranked". The code does not do what the doc says.

I rate it P2, not P1. Nothing under src/ builds `ScientificEvaluation` or calls `Experiment.best`, so it is reachable only through the public API. The original CORE-015 was itself P2. Cases A and B are plausible honest producer mistakes that the fix silently lets through, which fits "silently weakens a guard". Cases C and D need records that are deliberately contradictory.
